  "title": "Ten Whistlegraphs — Highlight Reel",
  "summary": "In Whistlegraph's first solo exhibition, the artist group (Jeffrey Scudder, Camille Klein, Alex Freundlich) presents a new body of work featuring creative documentation of \"whistlegraphs\" — an art form of scored musical drawings. Whistlegraphs are memorizable lyrical poems that double as graphic scores, resulting in reproducible drawings. \n\nBlending drawing with live performance documentation, Whistlegraph’s work evades simple capture. Each of the ten whistlegraphs curated for Feral File includes a high-definition video performance and graphic score made for the exhibition, plus a curated selection of TikToks accessible through a custom interactive player. Each piece generates countless media artifacts, from social media posts and works on paper to live recitals. Part of the beauty is that once you grok it, you can make a whistlegraph yourself; many fans interact with the group's work through duets, mashups, and sing-alongs to the tune of digital folk.\n\nLike Harold and the Purple Crayon, Whistlegraph draws a world into existence, reminding us that the inner child's creative light needn't be dimmed. Friendship radiates through the trio's work, whether colorful chalk art or choreographed iPhone performances. Yet Whistlegraph is also hip to Fluxus and contemporary visual art traditions that wrestle with the abstract qualities of graphical notation or art as language. In their practice, they dwell in the noise of moving from analog to digital and back again. \n\n***\nIn an exclusive Feral File \"<a href=\"https://feralfile.com/journal/close-up/whistlegraph-doodling-clockwork-in-lore-and-score\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Close-Up</a>\" text accompanying the exhibition, writer Dorothy Rose Howard discusses Whistlegraph's musical vernacular and digital folklore.",
